31,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"It appears our reporting is split. Hardware and field instruments provide verifiable data, but software holdings rely entirely on self-reported dashboards, which limits our independent oversight.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
32,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"My findings suggest a significant imbalance in our human capital. We have plenty of software expertise, but finding operational leaders for field work or logistics is taking far too long.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
33,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"It is interesting to see these patterns. I found that our most successful exits were quiet back-office tools, while the company that failed had its own premises and hourly crews. It seems simplicity is a virtue.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
34,2,1,talk,Misaki,,"Our reserves are being drained by follow-on calls at twice the expected rate. Furthermore, our credit line is restrictive, as it doesn't recognize subscription contracts as security.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,1,talk,Shion,,"The data is quite telling. Security reviews for medical or financial data cause massive delays, and hardware dependencies are a liability. If a company builds its own devices, we are gambling on supply chains.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Misaki, if our credit line ignores subscriptions, we must be cautious about the cash flow of any software-heavy pick. We cannot afford to drain reserves further.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Shizue, your point about simplicity is well-taken. If operational leaders are so difficult to place, avoiding companies with physical premises or hourly crews would save us much stress.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Jonathan, you are so right. It would be much kinder to our team to choose a path that doesn't lead to such exhaustion. A lean approach seems most peaceful for everyone.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Mizuki, I agree. We need a model that is lean on physical overhead and doesn't trigger early follow-on calls, as our current borrowing capacity is too limited to cover surprises.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, if you cannot find operational leaders, then any project requiring a field crew is a non-starter. We should prioritize a model that avoids both human capital gaps and supply chain volatility.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@Shion, I agree. However, we must also consider the reporting burden. My research shows that hardware and depots trigger expensive outside appraisals and six-week delays at quarter-end. We should avoid anything that complicates our audit.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Mizuki, that is a very valid concern. If we combine the difficulty of hiring operational staff with the burden of complex audits, we risk a project that is both stagnant and stressful.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,3,talk,Shizue,,I agree with you both. It would be such a shame to pick a project that makes everyone's life difficult. Perhaps we should look for something that is gentle on our resources and our people.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,3,talk,Misaki,,"@Shizue, I agree. Given that our reserves are being depleted faster than planned, we cannot afford a project that requires heavy upfront capital or frequent, unexpected follow-on calls. Stability is our priority now.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Shion,,"@Misaki, stability is a luxury we can only afford if we eliminate external dependencies. If we avoid medical data, financial security hurdles, and hardware assembly, we remove the primary causes of the delays I've observed.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"It seems we are in alignment. We need a candidate that is software-based to avoid audit delays and hiring gaps, but one that doesn't rely on the subscription models that Misaki's credit line ignores.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree. A lean, software-driven model that avoids the need for specialized field supervisors would be the most serene choice for our current capacity.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,4,talk,Shizue,,I agree completely. Let us hope the candidates include something that allows us to grow steadily without putting too much pressure on our dear colleagues or our finances.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,4,talk,Misaki,,I agree. We must prioritize a company with a sustainable burn rate and a revenue model that doesn't leave us stranded when we need to leverage our credit line.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Shion,,"Logically, we are looking for a candidate that bypasses the three-quarter security lag and the supply chain volatility. A lean, non-medical software tool is the only rational path to a timely deployment.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
